{
  "text": "```systemverilog\nmodule edge_propagator (\n  input  logic clk_tx_i,\n  input  logic rstn_tx_i,\n  input  logic edge_i,\n  input  logic clk_rx_i,\n  input  logic rstn_rx_i,\n  output logic edge_o\n);\n\n  // State to track the last value of edge_i in the TX domain\n  logic tx_edge_last;\n  \n  // State to track the last value of edge_o in the RX domain\n  logic rx_edge_last;\n\n  // TX Domain: Detect edges on edge_i\n  // We sample edge_i and detect transitions.\n  // On a rising edge of edge_i (or falling if we want to catch both, \n  // but typically \"edge\" implies rising unless specified otherwise. \n  // However, to catch any pulse/edge reliably without double counting, \n  // we usually detect the transition from 0->1 and then wait for 1->0 to reset the flag).\n  // To ensure we catch a single pulse regardless of width, we detect the rising edge.\n  // If the requirement is to catch both rising and falling edges as separate events, \n  // the logic would differ. Given \"pulse or edge\", detecting the start of the event (rising) \n  // and ensuring we don't re-trigger until the signal returns to low is the standard approach \n  // for single-shot propagation.\n  \n  always_ff @(posedge clk_tx_i or negedge rstn_tx_i) begin\n    if (!rstn_tx_i) begin\n      tx_edge_last <= 1'b0;\n    end else begin\n      // Detect rising edge\n      if (edge_i && !tx_edge_last) begin\n        tx_edge_last <= 1'b1;\n      end else begin\n        // If edge_i goes low, we are ready for the next edge\n        tx_edge_last <= 1'b0;\n      end\n    end\n  end\n\n  // RX Domain: Propagate the detected edge to the output\n  // We need to latch the 'tx_edge_last' signal across the clock domain boundary.\n  // Since we only care about the transition, we can use a simple flip-flop chain \n  // or a single register if we are careful about metastability and timing.\n  // A single register is sufficient if we assume the TX domain logic stabilizes \n  // before the RX clock samples it, or if we use a multi-stage synchronizer.\n  // For robustness against asynchronous clocks, a 2-stage synchronizer is standard practice.\n  \n  logic sync_edge_1;\n  logic sync_edge_2;\n\n  // Synchronizer for the TX edge detection signal\n  always_ff @(posedge clk_rx_i or negedge rstn_rx_i) begin\n    if (!rstn_rx_i) begin\n      sync_edge_1 <= 1'b0;\n      sync_edge_2 <= 1'b0;\n    end else begin\n      sync_edge_1 <= tx_edge_last;\n      sync_edge_2 <= sync_edge_1;\n    end\n  end\n\n  // Generate the output pulse\n  // We detect the rising edge of the synchronized signal.\n  // Since sync_edge_2 is the stable version of the TX edge, we detect its transition.\n  always_ff @(posedge clk_rx_i or negedge rstn_rx_i) begin\n    if (!rstn_rx_i) begin\n      edge_o <= 1'b0;\n    end else begin\n      // Detect rising edge of the synchronized signal\n      if (sync_edge_2 && !sync_edge_1) begin\n        edge_o <= 1'b1;\n      end else begin\n        edge_o <= 1'b0;\n      end\n    end\n  end\n\nendmodule\n```<|im_end|>\n<|endoftext|>",
